        "title": "Tarsal Tunnel Syndrome",
        "desc": "Compression of the tibial nerve in the tarsal tunnel at the inner ankle. Typical: tingling, numbness and pain in the sole of the foot and toes.",
        "modalSubtitle": "When a nerve becomes compressed at the inner ankle",
        "modalContent": "### What is Tarsal Tunnel Syndrome?\nTingling, burning, or numbness in the sole of the foot can be caused by nerve compression at the inner ankle. In tarsal tunnel syndrome, the tibial nerve or one of its branches is compressed or irritated within the tarsal tunnel, impairing normal nerve function.\n\nTypical complaints include tingling, burning sensations, numbness, or pain in the sole of the foot and toes. These symptoms frequently arise or worsen during weight-bearing activities or after prolonged standing and walking.\n\n### Diagnostics and Treatment\nAccurate diagnosis relies on a thorough medical history and neurological examination. Depending on findings, electrophysiological examinations (such as nerve conduction tests) and imaging techniques may be utilized to pinpoint the cause and rule out conditions with similar symptoms.\n\nTreatment is guided by the underlying cause and the severity of symptoms. Conservative measures are usually considered first. In cases of pronounced nerve compression and persistent discomfort, surgical decompression of the nerve may be indicated. Together with you, we discuss and determine the most appropriate individual treatment path.",
