        "desc": "Chronic pain is often accompanied by psychological comorbidities. A holistic approach including psychosomatic aspects improves therapy outcomes.",
        "modalSubtitle": "The connection between body, mind and chronic pain",
        "modalContent": "### Chronic pain – more than a physical problem\nChronic pain affects not just the body. Long-lasting complaints can also impair sleep, mood, concentration, performance and quality of life. At the same time, stress, anxiety, depressive symptoms or persistent emotional strain can influence the perception and processing of pain.\n\nThis does not mean that the pain is \"only psychological\" or imagined. Rather, physical, psychological and social factors in chronic pain are often closely interrelated.\n\nThat is why we approach chronic pain as holistically as possible. In addition to physical and neurological diagnostics, we also take psychosomatic accompanying factors and individual life circumstances into account.\n\nDepending on the findings, psychosomatic or psychotherapeutic treatment approaches may also be beneficial alongside medication, physiotherapy or interventional procedures.\n\nOur goal is not only to treat the pain itself, but also to reduce its impact on daily life, improve physical and psychological resilience, and thus support quality of life in the long term.",
